CM_ BO_ 2147909648 "
                Error codes currently raised by the charge controller. One bit per code, several
                can be set at once. Codes are named after DIN DKE SPEC 99003 wherever the
                specification defines one.

                Sent every second, and immediately whenever a code is raised or cleared, so
                the codes currently raised can be read at any time rather than only at the moment
                they change.

                How long a code stays raised is one of two behaviours, chosen per pistol in
                the controller's config file:

                - **Kept until the next session**, the default. A code outlives the session
                  that raised it: it stays raised through the return to idle and is cleared
                  only when the next plug-in starts a new session. 
                  whenever __Advantics_Controller_Status.State__ reads __Waiting_For_PEV__,
                  a code set here belongs to the session that has just finished. It tells you
                  why that session ended, not that something is wrong with the charger right now.
                - **Cleared on the way back to idle**, with the config entry 
                  `clear_error_codes_on_idle = true` in the pistol section.
                  A code is dropped as soon as the controller cycles
                  back to __Waiting_For_PEV__, so it is never visible outside the session
                  that raised it.

                Live status, such as which limit the charge is currently held to, is in
                <<Charger_Status_Flags>> instead, so a listener of these codes is never woken up
                by a status change.

                The pilot-line and V2G codes only apply to CCS and MCS sessions, and the __EV*__
                ones report what the vehicle declared about itself in DC_EVStatus.EVErrorCode
                (DIN SPEC 70121 and DIN EN ISO 15118-2 only).
            ";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 Reserved_Flags "Reserved bits for future uses.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 PowerPermitWithdrawn "The vehicle stopped granting its hardware power permit while power transfer was
                    in progress. Not reported when the permit drops at the normal end of
                    a charge.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 SessionStoppedBeforeCharge "The session ended before the charge phase started: the vehicle was connected and
                    communicating, but the charger never delivered power. Says that a
                    session was lost early, not why -- any code raised alongside it is
                    the reason. On its own it means the vehicle or the operator ended
                    it, or the negotiation never completed.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 EVChargingSystemIncompatibility "Vehicle reports DC_EVStatus.EVErrorCode = FAILED_ChargingSystemIncompatibility.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 EVChargingVoltageOutOfRange "Vehicle reports DC_EVStatus.EVErrorCode = FAILED_ChargingVoltageOutOfRange.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 EVChargingCurrentDifferential "Vehicle reports DC_EVStatus.EVErrorCode = FAILED_ChargingCurrentdifferential.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 EVRESSMalfunction "Vehicle reports DC_EVStatus.EVErrorCode = FAILED_EVRESSMalfunction.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 EVConnectorLockFault "Vehicle reports DC_EVStatus.EVErrorCode = FAILED_ChargerConnectorLockFault.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 EVShiftPosition "Vehicle reports DC_EVStatus.EVErrorCode = FAILED_EVShiftPosition.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 EVRESSTemperatureInhibit "Vehicle reports DC_EVStatus.EVErrorCode = FAILED_RESSTemperatureInhibit.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 CommunicationSetupTimeout "The vehicle did not complete the session setup within the time allowed by
                    `communication_setup_timeout`, counted from the communication link coming up.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 V2GProtocolNotAllowed "The negotiated application protocol is disabled, or not allowed over this connection.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 V2GParameterNotSupported "The application protocol the vehicle asked for is not supported.
                    DIN DKE SPEC 99003 use case 8.4.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 V2GSessionIDUnexpected "The vehicle used a session ID we do not know.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 V2GSequenceError "The vehicle sent a request that is not allowed in the current sequence state.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 ChargeEnableStateUnexpected "Charge Enable is in a valid state, but not one the charging sequence allows at that
                    point, such as leaving C while power is being transferred or dropping
                    to A with a session still running. Readings the sequence treats as
                    noise are not reported.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 ChargeEnableFault "The MCS Charge Enable line is in error state E. The line itself is at fault.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 ControlPilotStateUnexpected "Control Pilot is in a valid state, but not one the charging sequence allows at that
                    point: B where C or D is required, C or D where only B is, or a drop
                    to A with a session still running. The normal unplug once a session
                    has ended is not reported.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 ControlPilotFault "The CCS Control Pilot line is in an error state: E (0 V) or F, the charger
                    signalling itself unavailable. The line itself is at fault.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 BidirectionalNotSupported "The vehicle requested discharge from a charger that is not bidirectional.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 RangeModeNotSupported "Range mode setpoints were requested from a charger configured without range mode.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 CANBusError "Sending on the power modules CAN bus failed.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 NodeDisconnected "A required controller application stopped answering its heartbeat.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 InternalError "An unhandled error occurred inside the controller.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 ChargerSequenceError "The power modules refused a state transition the controller asked for.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 HighTemperature "A monitored temperature crossed its charge-stop threshold.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 PowerModuleRampDownTimeout "The power modules did not bring the output current down in time when the charge
                    ended. The contactors were left closed.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 PowerModuleDisabled "The power modules withdrew Power_Modules_Status.System_Enable during a powered phase.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 PowerModuleCANTimeout "No Power_Modules_Status received within the configured timeout.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 PowerModuleFault "The power modules are in a fault or defective state.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 InsulationMonitorFault "The insulation monitoring device reports an internal device error. Distinct from
                    InsulationFault, which is the measured resistance being too low.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 InsulationFault "Insulation resistance is below the allowed limit.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 ConnectorLockFailure "The connector lock driver reports a fault. AC charging only.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 OutputDriverFault "The industrial digital output driver reports a fault.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 EmergencyStopLoopOpen "The emergency stop current loop is open.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909648 InterlockFault "The interlock protection input is pulled low. Any safety device wired to that
                    line can pull it down to stop the charge. Causes that have their own code
                    are excluded here: the emergency stop loop, the industrial output driver,
                    and the controller pulling the line low itself.";
CM_ BO_ 2147909649 "
                Live status flags of the charge controller. One bit per flag, several can be set
                at once. During a charge session, a flag can be set then goes back to 0 on its own
                once its condition is gone.

                Sent every second, and immediately whenever a flag changes.

                - When two limits give the same value, both flags are set.
                - The same flags cover charging and discharging, and in range mode they cover
                  both ends of the range at once.
                - A power limit becomes a current limit once divided by the present voltage, so
                  which limit is the lowest can change as the voltage moves. Several frames may
                  follow in quick succession when it does.
            ";
CM_ SG_ 2147909649 Reserved_Flags "Reserved bits for future uses.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909649 VehicleCurrentLimit "The maximum current the vehicle declared is what holds the current down, below every
                    limit of our own. DC only.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909649 OCPPCurrentLimit "The limit set by OCPP smart charging is what holds the current down, either as a
                    current or as a power divided by the present voltage. Applies to charging, not
                    discharging.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909649 CableTemperatureDerating "A monitored temperature is derating the cable's current limit. Set whenever that
                    derating applies, whether or not it is what holds the current down.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909649 CableVoltageLimit "The cable's own rated voltage (`max_cable_voltage`) is what holds the target voltage
                    below what the vehicle asked for. DC only.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909649 CableCurrentLimit "The cable's own rating is what holds the current down: `max_cable_current`, or
                    `max_cable_power` divided by the present voltage. On AC,
                    `max_cable_phase_current`.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909649 ChargerTemperatureDerating "A monitored temperature is derating the charger's current limit. Set whenever that
                    derating applies, whether or not it is what holds the current down: it is a real
                    reduction of what the charger can do.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909649 ChargerVoltageLimit "The charger's own rated voltage (`max_charger_voltage`, or a lower limit the power
                    modules report) is what holds the target voltage below what the vehicle asked
                    for. DC only.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909649 ChargerCurrentLimit "The charger's own rating is what holds the current down: `max_charger_current`,
                    `max_charger_power` divided by the present voltage, their discharge
                    counterparts, or a lower limit the power modules report. On AC,
                    `max_charger_phase_current`.";
CM_ BO_ 2147909650 "
                What the insulation monitor the charge controller reads itself is measuring.

                Sent every second, and only when the controller is configured to read a supported
                insulation monitor. A charger that measures the insulation itself, and reports it
                in <<Power_Modules_Status.Insulation_Resistance>>, never sees this message: the
                value is already its own.

                This is a reading, not a verdict. The outcome of the insulation test is the charge
                sequence moving on, and a failure is reported as __InsulationFault__ or
                __InsulationMonitorFault__ in <<Charger_Diagnostic_Status>>.
            ";
CM_ SG_ 2147909650 Reserved "Reserved bits for future uses.";
CM_ SG_ 2147909650 Device_Status "
                    What the device says about itself and about the resistance it measures,
                    ordered from no news to worst news.

                    The first two are the controller's own answers rather than the device's,
                    and are the two that mean there is no measurement:

                    - __Not_Available__: no insulation monitor is being read at all. It cannot
                      appear on this message, which is only sent when one is.
                    - __No_Communication__: a monitor is configured and is not answering --
                      not yet, or not any more. A silent device is not a faulty one, so this is
                      not reported as __Device_Error__.

                    __Prewarning__, __Warning__ and __Alarm__ are the device telling you about
                    the resistance, and the measurement stands. __Device_Error__ is the device
                    reporting a fault of its own, and then there is no measurement: on a device
                    error this ISOMETER re-purposes its measurement channels to report earth
                    and system connection instead of resistance and voltage.
                ";
CM_ SG_ 2147909650 Voltage "
                    Voltage measured by the device on the line it watches. This is its own
                    measurement, independent of the charger's, so it can be compared against
                    <<Power_Modules_Status.Present_Voltage>>.

                    65535 means there is no measurement to report.
                ";
CM_ SG_ 2147909650 Resistance "
                    Insulation resistance measured by the device.

                    65535 means there is no measurement to report, which is also what
                    __Device_Status__ then says. It is never reported as 0, which would read as a
                    dead short.
                ";
CM_ BO_ 2147909651 "
